# Commits this file's `git blame` should look straight through.
#
# M6.6a rewrote comment prose in 166 files, punctuation only, and left every code
# file byte-identical once comments were blanked. M6.6b then renamed 86 British
# identifiers, so its entry is the first here whose code bytes really did move.
# Without this list, blame on any of those files points at the sweep instead of at
# whoever wrote the line, and the reason a trap was documented gets one step harder
# to find.
#
# Enable it locally with:
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
# GitHub reads it from the repository root without being asked.
#
# Only add a commit here that changed no behavior. A commit that mixes a rewrite
# with a real change does not belong, because ignoring it hides the real change.
#
# ⚠ M6.6b's entry stretches that rule and the stretch is deliberate, so here is the
# reasoning rather than a silent exception. That commit also adds
# tools/m6.6b-spelling.py and rewrites tools/m6.6-guard.py, which are real changes.
# They are safe to ignore anyway: --ignore-rev works per line, and for a line whose
# only commit is the ignored one, git falls back to attributing it there. So the
# tooling keeps its blame while the 133 swept files hand theirs back. If a future
# entry mixes a rewrite with a change to a file the rewrite also touched, that one
# genuinely does not belong.
#
# M6.6a's own final commit, 1b60668, is deliberately NOT listed. It mixed the
# heading and en dash sweep with a fix to the scanner's escaping and with restoring
# four strings the sweep had broken, and those restorations are exactly the lines a
# reader would want blame to point at.

